## 5. Residual identity, constants and arbitrary references

Write delta0=1/2 and v=||V||. In eigenbases of H0|Q and K, the Sylvester inverse divides each matrix entry by a number of modulus at least delta0. Its Frobenius-norm bound is therefore 1/delta0. Since the input dimension is four, ||VJ||F<=2v. It follows that

\[
\|S_1\|\le 2v/\delta_0,\qquad
\|S_2\|\le 2v^2/\delta_0^2,\qquad
\|H_2\|\le2v^2/\delta_0.
\]

The S2 bound uses ||VS1||F<=v||S1||F; multiplying an operator-norm bound by another unnecessary dimension factor would be an incorrect way to reproduce it. With F=J+S1+S2, expansion and PVS1=JH2 give the exact full-space identity

\[
(H_0+V)F-F(K+H_2)=VS_2-S_1H_2-S_2H_2=R.
\]

Thus

\[
\|R\|\le6v^3/\delta_0^2+4v^4/\delta_0^3.
\]

Adding lambda I to both generators leaves this residual unchanged. Both generators are Hermitian; F need not be an isometry. Duhamel applied to F, followed by the two endpoint replacements F→J, gives

\[
\|e^{-it(H_0+V+\lambda)}J-Je^{-it(K+H_2+\lambda)}\|
\le4v/\delta_0+4v^2/\delta_0^2+t\|R\|.
